   "source": [
    "# Google Play Apps and Reviews: Exploratory Data Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1. Project title and introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "**Course:** CSB314 - Exploratory Data Analysis  \n",
    "**Assessment:** Minor Project 02 (CA 03)\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook explores the supplied Google Play app catalog and user-review files. It checks the data, creates useful app-level features, summarizes the catalog, aggregates reviews before integration, and applies descriptive and statistical methods from the project brief.\n",
    "\n",
    "The app catalog has one or more rows per app name, and the review table has many rows per app. The grain of each table is checked before joining. Review data is summarized to one row per app before it is merged into the app catalog, which prevents review rows from multiplying app records.\n",
    "\n",
    "Results describe the supplied dataset snapshot. They do not establish current app availability, current installs, or causal effects. Missing values, repeated rows, and limitations are documented in the relevant sections."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "34b18b60",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Problem and objectives\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal is to understand the app catalog and review information in the supplied files. The analysis focuses on app categories, ratings, installs, prices, genres, update dates, and review sentiment.\n",
    "\n",
    "The work follows the project topics: data-quality checks, feature engineering, grouping and transformation, pivot tables and crosstabs, reshaping, integration, descriptive statistics, distribution and sampling analysis, correlation, and outlier assessment.\n",
    "\n",
    "The central integration question is whether app-level attributes and summarized review sentiment can be compared without double-counting apps. To answer it, the notebook checks keys and aggregates the many review rows to app level before merging."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "11b0f1bc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Dataset source and description\n",
    "\n",
    "The supplied files are `googleplaystore.csv` and `googleplaystore_user_reviews.csv`. The assignment lists the [Google Play Store Apps and Reviews dataset on Kaggle](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/lava18/google-play-store-apps). The accompanying `license.txt` states that the work is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 Unported. Keep the source files and licence beside the notebook.\n",
    "\n",
    "The app catalog contains app name, category, rating, review count, size, install band, price, content rating, genres, update date, and version fields. The review table contains app name, translated review text, sentiment label, sentiment polarity, and subjectivity.\n",
    "\n",
    "The app name is the available link between the tables. Since app names repeat in the catalog, they must be checked and reduced to one app row before a one-to-one join with app-level review summaries."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bf8033f9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
